  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The solution to $\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{6}{y}$ is$\\,\\,y\\,=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nTo solve the equation, we first apply cross-multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{5} & =\\frac{6}{y} \\\\ 2\\cdot y & =6\\cdot 5 \\\\ 2y & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the multiplication principle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y & =30 \\\\ \\frac{2y}{2} & =\\frac{30}{2} \\\\ y & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince  $y=15$ is different from $y=0,$ we can accept it as a solution. Thus, the solution is $y=15.$",
